 farming communities of Europe. In Farmers or Hunter-gatherers? Peter Sutton and Keryn Walshe ask why Australians have been so receptive to the notion that farming represents an advance from hunting and gathering. Drawing on the knowledge of Aboriginal elders, previously not included within this discussion, and decades of anthropological scholarship, Sutton and Walshe provide extensive evidence to support their argument that classical Aboriginal society was a hunter-gatherer society and as sophisticated as the traditional European farming methods. Farmers or Hunter-gatherers? asks Australians to develop a deeper understanding and appreciation of Aboriginal society and culture.","displaySummary":"In Farmers or Hunter-gatherers? Peter Sutton and Keryn Walshe ask why Australians have been so receptive to the notion that farming represents an advance from hunting and gathering. Drawing on the knowledge of Aboriginal elders, previously not included within this discussion, and decades of anthropological scholarship, Sutton and Walshe provide extensive evidence to support their argument that classical Aboriginal society was a hunter-gatherer society and as sophisticated as the traditional European farming methods. Farmers or Hunter-gatherers? asks Australians to develop a deeper understanding and appreciation of Aboriginal society and culture.","primarySubject":"Arts & Criticism","primaryTopic":"Essays & Cultural Criticism","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Arts & Criticism","confidence":"low","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Arts & Criticism","score":4,"evidenceCount":2},{"subject":"World History","score":3,"evidenceCount":2},{"subject":"History","score":3,"evidenceCount":3},{"subject":"Society & Culture","score":2,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"keyword_classifier","rawLabel":"Arts & Criticism","mappedSubject":"Arts & Criticism","score":2,"confidence":"low","note":"world history keyword; history keyword or history prize; society/culture k